2. Initialize environment and Express
dotenv.config();
const app = express();


dotenv.config() reads .env and adds each variable (like GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID) into process.env.

const app = express() creates your web server application.

From now on, you’ll attach routes and middleware to this app.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Middleware configuration

Parse incoming requests
app.use(express.json());
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));


express.json() allows Express to read JSON request bodies.

express.urlencoded() lets it read data from HTML forms (<form method="POST">).

Without these, req.body would be empty in your POST routes.

Configure sessions
app.use(
  session({
    secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,
    resave: false,
    saveUninitialized: true,
  })
);


This tells Express to:

Create a session cookie (stored in the browser).

Keep session data on the server, so each user can have their own login state.

Key options:

secret: used to encrypt the cookie so others can’t fake it.

resave: false: don’t re-save unchanged sessions.

saveUninitialized: true: create a session even if it’s empty.

Think of the session as a “memory slot” on the server for each user’s data.

Enable Passport
app.use(passport.initialize());
app.use(passport.session());


These two lines connect Passport to Express and allow it to use session support.

passport.initialize() sets up the Passport system.

passport.session() plugs Passport into the session system so users stay logged in after authenticating once.

