🪪 5. Passport session handling
passport.serializeUser((user, done) => done(null, user.id));
passport.deserializeUser((id, done) => done(null, users.get(id)));

Why this matters:

When a user logs in, Passport needs to remember them across requests.

Serialization: store the user’s unique ID inside the session cookie.

Saves space — we don’t store the whole user object in the cookie.

Deserialization: when the user returns, Passport reads the ID and looks them up in the users map.

Think of this as “putting the user’s ID in a name tag” when they log in, and “looking them up in the guest list” when they return.

